## TLDR Problem this solves: - Semantic cache embeds the full prompt, no truncation lever exists - Prompts over the embedding model's limit fail silently, cache never hits - `extra_body` on embedding deployments is sent as a literal nested key - Strict OpenAI-compatible servers (vLLM, NIM) reject it with 400 extra_forbidden How it solves it: - Embedding requests now merge `extra_body` into the body, like chat does - Semantic cache truncates embedding input to the deployment's `max_input_tokens` - New `cache_params.semantic_cache_embedding_max_input_tokens` overrides that per config - Applies to redis-semantic, valkey-semantic, and qdrant-semantic backends ## User Flow Before: a proxy admin points the semantic cache at a vLLM embedding deployment whose server enforces a small token limit, and the cache never hits while direct embedding calls fail outright 1. The admin configures `litellm_settings.cache_params` with `type: redis-semantic` and `redis_semantic_cache_embedding_model: vllm-embed`, where the `vllm-embed` deployment carries `extra_body: {truncate: END, input_type: query}` for its OpenAI-compatible server, then starts the proxy 2. A developer sends POST http://localhost:4000/v1/embeddings with `{"model": "vllm-embed", "input": "<long text>"}` and gets HTTP 400 back: `extra_forbidden ... ('body', 'extra_body') ... Extra inputs are not permitted`, because the params were sent as a literal nested `extra_body` key instead of at the top level 3. The developer sends the same long-prompt POST http://localhost:4000/v1/chat/completions twice; both return HTTP 200 with full provider cost and no `x-litellm-cache-key` header, because every cache-lookup embedding call fails the same way behind the scenes 4. Nothing in `cache_params` or the deployment can cap the prompt before it is embedded, so with providers that hard-limit input (OpenAI embeddings at 8192 tokens) every long prompt stays a permanent cache miss while short prompts hit fine After: the same config works end to end, long prompts get semantic cache hits 1. The admin keeps the exact same config and starts the proxy 2. The developer sends POST http://localhost:4000/v1/embeddings with `{"model": "vllm-embed", "input": "<long text>"}` and gets HTTP 200 with an embedding back, because `truncate` and `input_type` now arrive at the top level of the request and the server truncates server-side 3. The developer sends the same long-prompt POST http://localhost:4000/v1/chat/completions twice; the second response returns `x-litellm-cache-key` and the identical cached completion (same response id) 4. For embedding models without a server-side truncate option, the admin sets `max_input_tokens` on the embedding deployment's `model_info` (or `semantic_cache_embedding_max_input_tokens` in `cache_params`) and the proxy truncates the prompt to that many tokens before embedding it, so the same second call returns `x-litellm-cache-key` and the cached completion ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-5527 ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Setup for both legs: proxy booted with `python litellm/proxy/proxy_cli.py --config <yaml> --port <port> --detailed_debug`, Redis 8 (bundles RediSearch) on port 6410. Config A: redis-semantic cache embedding via an OpenAI `text-embedding-3-small` deployment with `model_info.max_input_tokens: 8191`, plus `nim-embed` = `hosted_vllm/nvidia/nv-embedqa-e5-v5` at `integrate.api.nvidia.com` with `extra_body: {truncate: END, input_type: query}`. Config B (the customer's exact shape): redis-semantic cache whose `redis_semantic_cache_embedding_model` is that `nim-embed` hosted_vllm deployment. Real OpenAI and NVIDIA NIM APIs, no mocks ### Before (6d32d4081dc5696d6be9307195afac3612d80de2) #### Case 1: /v1/embeddings on a hosted_vllm deployment carrying extra_body ``` $ curl -s -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" http://localhost:44127/v1/embeddings \ -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d '{"model": "nim-embed", "input": "<826-token text>"}' {"error":{"message":"litellm.BadRequestError: Hosted_vllmException - {"error":"Your request cannot be validated, it is incorrect. detail={'type': 'extra_forbidden', 'loc': ('body', 'extra_body'), 'msg': 'Extra inputs are not permitted', 'input': {'truncate': 'END', 'input_type': 'query'}}"}", ...}} HTTP 400 ``` The deployment's `extra_body` params were sent as a literal nested `extra_body` key, which the strict OpenAI-compatible server rejects #### Case 2: semantic cache over the hosted_vllm embedding deployment (config B, port 44131) ``` $ for i in 1 2; do curl -s -D - -o /dev/null http://localhost:44131/v1/chat/completions \ -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d @lit5527_nim_chat.json | grep -Ei "HTTP|x-litellm-cache-key|x-litellm-response-cost"; done HTTP/1.1 200 OK x-litellm-response-cost: 0.0001982 HTTP/1.1 200 OK x-litellm-response-cost: 0.00019195 ``` No `x-litellm-cache-key` on either call and both are billed: every cache-lookup embedding call fails on the nested `extra_body`, so the semantic cache never hits #### Case 3: prompt over the embedding model's token limit (config A, 9795-token chat prompt) ``` $ for i in 1 2; do curl -s -D - -o /dev/null http://localhost:44127/v1/chat/completions \ -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d @lit5527_long_chat.json | grep -Ei "HTTP|x-litellm-cache-key|x-litellm-response-cost"; done HTTP/1.1 200 OK x-litellm-response-cost: 0.0019977 HTTP/1.1 200 OK x-litellm-response-cost: 0.0003749 ``` No `x-litellm-cache-key` on either call: OpenAI rejects the 9795-token embedding input ("maximum context length is 8192 tokens") behind the scenes, nothing can truncate it, and the failure is silent. A short-prompt control on the same proxy did hit (`x-litellm-cache-key` on call 3), proving the cache itself works ### After (ef2c30227a) Same configs, proxies rebooted from this branch, Redis indexes dropped first so every pair starts cold #### Case 1: /v1/embeddings on the hosted_vllm deployment carrying extra_body ``` $ curl -s -w "
